// @METADATA:name        = hur_motivating
// @METADATA:description = Motivating example from Hur et al. (2014) Figure 1.
//                         Shows classical slicing is unsound for probabilistic
//                         programs. A classical slicer removes observe(y=1)
//                         since y has no data dep on x, but this changes the
//                         posterior of x. The key soundness argument.
// @METADATA:reference   = Hur Nori Rajamani Samuel (2014) Slicing
//                         Probabilistic Programs. PLDI 2014
// @METADATA:criterion   = x
// @METADATA:expected    = z sliced out; observe(y=1) MUST be kept despite
//                         y not in classical data dep of x; y kept via
//                         observe dependence; KEY soundness example
// @METADATA:tags        = observe, soundness, classical-vs-probabilistic,
//                         key-example, hur-pldi14

x :~ bernoulli(0.5);
y :~ bernoulli(0.5);
observe(y = 1);
z := 42;
return x;